<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Life Adventures</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Small, Brown Bag.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the main plot of simple stories told with visual support. Interactive: Demonstrate comprehension of a variety of classroom commands.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow the main idea of a simple story and identify familiar vocabulary when visuals are provided (e.g., a story about a small red pencil and a big blue book going to school, with students identifying them as the teacher points to the corresponding pictures while asking, "What size is the pencil?"). Can understand and respond appropriately to basic classroom commands (e.g., "Please sit down." or "Open your book."). Can participate in brief exchanges using simple, familiar classroom phrases (e.g., responding to "What color is the bag?" with "It's brown.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a "Hello Song" incorporating movements (e.g., "Hello, hello, how are you?"). Then, the teacher uses real classroom objects (a book, a bag, a pencil) and flashcards to introduce or review vocabulary. The teacher holds up objects one by one and says, "This is a book. What is this?" encouraging choral repetition and single-word answers. Students practice greetings: "How are you?" "I'm fine, thank you."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words: "book" (/b/), "small" (/s/), "pencil" (/p/) with clear articulation and exaggerated movements (e.g., making a 'small' gesture with hands, 'big' with arms). The teacher points to a small, brown bag and says clearly, "This is a small, brown bag." Then points to a big, blue book and says, "That's a big, blue book." The teacher uses TPR by having students follow commands like "Please stand up," "Please sit down," "Open your book," "Close your book," modeling the appropriate actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the target vocabulary and concepts (size, color, object identity) using simple questions and gestures.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a small bag) "Is it big or small?" (Expected answer: "Small.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a red pencil) "What color is the pencil?" (Expected answer: "Red.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a book) "Is this a bag?" (Expected answer: "No, it's a book.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Life Adventures")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simple, illustrated story or a short audio recording (1-2 minutes) about "School Life Adventures" using large, colorful flashcards or a projected storybook. The narrative focuses on classroom objects, their sizes, and colors. The language is simple, repetitive, and targets Pre-A1.4 CEFR level. For example, "This is a small, brown bag. And this is a small, red pencil. The small, red pencil is in the brown bag. Look! A big, blue book is on the desk. The small, brown bag and the big, blue book go to school."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the main plot of simple stories told with visual support. Receptive: Demonstrate comprehension of a variety of classroom commands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the story while looking at the visuals. The teacher reads clearly, pointing to corresponding pictures.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What is the story about?" (Expected: "A bag and a book." or "School.") The teacher prompts with pictures. "Is it about animals or school things?" (Expected: "School things.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identification:</b> "Point to the small, brown bag." "Point to the big, blue book." "Point to the red pencil." (Teacher monitors students' pointing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual Check):</b> (Teacher shows a picture of a small, brown bag, then says) "That's a big, red bag." (Students shake heads or say "No.") (Teacher shows a picture of a blue book, then says) "That's a blue book." (Students nod or say "Yes.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening to identify specific classroom objects, their sizes, and colors. Grammar focus: Adjectives (big, small, brown, red, blue), Present simple (That's a...), WH-questions (What color...?, What size...?), Pronoun (it), Demonstratives (this, that), Prepositions (in, on). Vocabulary: book, bag, pencil, desk, chair, red, blue, brown, small, big.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Point (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher lays out flashcards of various classroom objects in different sizes and colors (e.g., a small red pencil, a big blue book, a brown bag). The teacher says sentences, and students listen and point to the correct flashcard. Examples: "Point to the small, red pencil." "Point to the big, blue book." "Point to the brown bag." (Teacher repeats each sentence 2-3 times as needed, ensuring all students are pointing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Identify (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard (e.g., a big, yellow crayon) and asks a WH-question, "What color is the crayon?" Students listen and respond chorally with one word, "Yellow!" or "It's yellow." Then, the teacher asks, "Is it big or small?" and students respond, "Big!" or "It's big." This is repeated with several items like "What size is the book?," "What color is the bag?".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen and Draw (Guided Production):</b> Students are given a piece of paper and crayons. The teacher gives simple commands, and students listen and draw what they hear. Examples: "Draw a small, red pencil." "Draw a big, blue book." "Draw a brown bag." The teacher walks around, observing students' drawings as they demonstrate their listening comprehension. (Phonemic awareness: The teacher emphasizes /p/ in pencil, /b/ in book, /s/ in small as they give instructions).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Life Adventures")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Classroom Command Chain.</b> This activity focuses on listening to and acting upon sequential commands within the "School Life Adventures" scenario, reinforcing the theme "That's a Small, Brown Bag."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher gives a series of 1-2 step instructions using target vocabulary and grammar. Students listen carefully and perform the actions. This is a whole-class activity where students demonstrate comprehension through physical responses (TPR).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher places various classroom objects (actual items or large cut-outs/flashcards) around the classroom (e.g., a small brown bag, a big blue book, a small red pencil, a yellow ruler).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives a command combining color, size, object, and preposition/verb. Example 1: "Please find the small, red pencil." (Students listen and retrieve the correct item).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "Take the big, blue book and put it on the desk." (Students listen, retrieve the book, and place it correctly).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 3: "Look for the brown bag and put the red pencil in it." (Students listen to a two-step command and execute).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher continues with 3-4 more commands, increasing complexity slightly (e.g., adding "next to" or "under"). Students respond individually or in small groups as instructed. Focus is on listening for details and executing the command. The teacher encourages polite expressions like "Thank you" when an item is handed over.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during the "Classroom Command Chain" and "Listen and Draw" activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will demonstrate their listening comprehension by accurately following commands involving classroom objects, colors, and sizes, and by correctly drawing objects based on descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identifies objects (Listening):</b> Can correctly point to/retrieve the named object (e.g., "book," "bag," "pencil").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Identifies attributes (Listening):</b> Can differentiate objects by size (big/small) and color (red, blue, brown, yellow) when described.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Follows commands (Interactive Listening):</b> Can perform 1-2 step classroom commands appropriately (e.g., "Put the small, red pencil in the brown bag.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic Use:</b> Shows understanding of simple classroom commands like "Please sit down."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Show a picture of a small, red pencil) "I can hear 'small' and 'red'?" (Students give a thumbs up or thumbs down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can follow the teacher's instructions?" (Thumbs up/down).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you hear and understand today?" (Encourage them to point to objects or say a word in L1 if needed).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to listen to? What was a little difficult?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific listening activities engaged students most effectively? Were the visuals and TPR sufficient for their understanding?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which vocabulary or grammar points need more reinforcement? Did all students demonstrate comprehension of the basic commands and story gist?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for differentiation (e.g., more repetition, simpler commands for some, more complex for others)?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The vocabulary (classroom objects, colors, sizes) and basic sentence structures ("That's a...", "It's a...") introduced through listening will be revisited in Lesson 2, which will focus on reading comprehension of a simple text using these same elements. Students will transition from listening to identifying the words and their meaning in a written context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>